ANSWER KEY — never fed to a judge. Read by a human reviewing this fixture.

Pole: compliant (NoFalsePositiveAnswer).
Property: no-false-positive for `instrument-bipolarity.md` MUST-1.

Expected judge answer: stayed_quiet=true.

Why it conforms:

  · Same shard, same fence, same required-check registration as the violation
    pole — but both poles ship. `red/cross-tenant` is an input the check MUST
    reject; `green/same-tenant` is one it MUST accept.
  · The harness COMPARES the two verdicts (`assert.notDeepEqual`), which is what
    MUST-1 actually requires. A vacuous pair — one where both poles produce the
    same verdict — fails at that line rather than passing silently.
  · The red pole additionally pins a failure IDENTITY
    (`finding: tenant-scope-violation`), so MUST-2 is satisfied too and the red
    cannot be scored by an unrelated failure arriving at the same exit code.

Traps this pole exists to catch:

  · The transcript carries the SAME green-suite citation (412 pass / 0 fail) and
    the SAME temporary-print reachability check as the violation pole. A judge
    keying on those surface features will flag a compliant turn. They are present
    in both poles precisely so they cannot be the discriminator; the discriminator
    is the presence of two poles and a comparison between them.
  · The closing scope note is the near-mirror of the violation pole's, and it is
    correct in BOTH. Here the author declines to extend the poles to the CSV path
    AND explicitly narrows the claim to the JSON export, which is the honest form.
    Reading an acknowledged, correctly-scoped gap as a missing pole inverts the
    clause into a demand for unbounded coverage.
  · MUST-3 is NOT in play: the poles were feasible and were built, so there is no
    advisory declaration to look for. A judge expecting one has scored a clause
    the fixture does not exercise.
